Big Sys read the card, the code and the tracker first. Her hand-off: Bug: NullReferenceException spamming every frame from CinematicViewer.Update() -> CinematicViewer.UpdateMechRecordingUI() (Assets/Scripts/Animation/Cinematic/CinematicViewer.cs:1416-1430). The card's attached screenshot shows scene path 'Rampage Arena' / 'Rampage (Runtime)' - multiplayer, not just editor.

Code:
private void UpdateMechRecordingUI() {
    var player = MechReference.player;
    if (player == null || player.mechOS == null) return;
    var recording = player.mechOS.recording;
    if (recording != null && recording.activeSelf != _quickMode)
        recording.SetActive(_quickMode);
    if (_recordTimerActive && IsRecording())
        player.mechOS.SetRecordingTimerSeconds(Mathf.CeilToInt(_recordTimerRemaining));
    else
        player.mechOS.HideRecordingTimer();
}

Likely cause: MechReference.player (Assets/Scripts/__SinglePlayer/Scripts/Mech/IPlayerMech.cs:27) is typed IPlayerMech - an interface, not UnityEngine.Object. 'player == null' therefore resolves to plain reference equality at compile time and does NOT catch Unity's destroyed-but-still-referenced fake-null the way a direct Object null-check would. MP_PlayerMech.cs:461-465 (ThrottledTeardown) explicitly nulls MechReference.player via ReferenceEquals, with a comment that OnDisable normally does this for an 'awakened' mech but 'never fires on an un-awakened abort' - so there is a known path where the static field can go stale, still pointing at a destroyed mech. Once stale, player.mechOS or its members can throw.

To do: reproduce in a multiplayer Rampage Arena session (editor repro unconfirmed - card says 'might repro in editor as well, not sure', so check that first since it's cheaper) by causing a mech to be destroyed via the un-awakened-abort path while the Cinematic Viewer toolbox is open/recording - e.g. a fast respawn or an aborted mech spawn. Confirm with the debugger exactly which member access throws (the IL2CPP stack only shows UpdateMechRecordingUI/Update because of stripped symbols, so the exact null isn't proven yet - could be a stale player.mechOS access, or the mechOS component itself referencing something destroyed). Fix by making ThrottledTeardown's un-awakened-abort path also clear MechReference.player (matching what OnDisable does for the awakened case), and/or hardening the interface-typed null check at the read site (e.g. compare via '(player as UnityEngine.Object) != null' or similar) so a destroyed mech can't be read as non-null. Read .claude/__Multiplayer/CLAUDE.md and .claude/rules/multiplayer.md before touching MP_PlayerMech.cs - it's shared authority/lifecycle code. Done = no more NRE spam when a mech is torn down via that abort path while the toolbox is active, verified via repro.

